1
00:00:01,480 --> 00:00:04,480
Charlotte.

2
00:00:06,519 --> 00:00:14,358
The night seemed long. Wilbur's stomach was empty and his mind was full. And

3
00:00:14,358 --> 00:00:20,838
when your stomach is empty and your mind is full, it's always hard to sleep.

4
00:00:20,838 --> 00:00:25,639
A dozen times during the night, Wilbur woke and stared into the blackness,

5
00:00:25,639 --> 00:00:30,920
listening to the sounds and trying to figure out what time it was.

6
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:39,079
A barn is never perfectly quiet. Even at midnight, there was usually something stirring.

7
00:00:39,079 --> 00:00:43,880
The first time he woke, he heard Templeton gnawing a hole in the grain

8
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:52,358
bin. Templeton's teeth scraped loudly against the wood and made quite a racket.

9
00:00:52,358 --> 00:00:58,679
That crazy rat, thought Wilbur. Why does he have to stay up all night grinding

10
00:00:58,679 --> 00:01:04,358
his clashers and destroying people's property? Why can't he go to sleep like

11
00:01:04,358 --> 00:01:10,199
any decent animal? The second time Wilbur woke, he heard the goose turning

12
00:01:10,199 --> 00:01:14,519
on her nest and chuckling to herself.

13
00:01:14,519 --> 00:01:19,719
"What time is it?" whispered Wilbur to the goose. "Probably, obly, probably

14
00:01:19,719 --> 00:01:26,119
about half 11," said the goose. "Why aren't you asleep, Wilbur?" "Too many

15
00:01:26,119 --> 00:01:33,719
things on my mind," said Wilbur. "Well," said the goose, "That's not my trouble.

16
00:01:33,719 --> 00:01:40,838
I have nothing at all on my mind, but I've too many things under my behind.

17
00:01:40,838 --> 00:01:46,599
Have you ever tried to sleep while sitting on eight eggs? No, replied

18
00:01:46,599 --> 00:01:52,000
Wilbur. I suppose it is uncomfortable.

19
00:01:52,199 --> 00:01:57,000
How long does it take a goose egg to hatch? Approximately approximately 30

20
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,760
days, all told, answered the goose. But I cheat a little on warm afternoons. I

21
00:02:04,760 --> 00:02:11,239
just pull a little straw over the eggs and go out for a walk. Wilbur yawned and

22
00:02:11,239 --> 00:02:17,639
went back to sleep. In his dreams, he heard again the voice saying, "I'll be a

23
00:02:17,639 --> 00:02:24,119
friend to you. Go to sleep. You'll see me in the morning."

24
00:02:24,119 --> 00:02:30,760
About half an hour before dawn, Wilbur woke and listened. The barn was still

25
00:02:30,760 --> 00:02:38,280
dark. The sheep lay motionless. Even the goose was quiet. Overhead on the main

26
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:45,719
floor, nothing stirred. The cows were resting. The horses dozed. Templeton had

27
00:02:45,719 --> 00:02:51,480
quit work and gone off somewhere on an errand. The only sound was a slight

28
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:57,880
scraping noise from the rooftop where the weather veins swung back and forth.

29
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:05,559
Wilbur loved the barn when it was like this, calm and quiet, waiting for light.

30
00:03:05,559 --> 00:03:08,920
"Day is almost here," he thought.

31
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:13,880
Through a small window, a faint gleam appeared.

32
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:20,760
One by one, the stars went out. Wilbur could see the goose a few feet away. She

33
00:03:20,760 --> 00:03:26,760
sat with head tucked under a wing. Then he could see the sheep and the lambs.

34
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:29,400
The sky lightened.

35
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:33,318
"Oh, beautiful day, it is here at last.

36
00:03:33,318 --> 00:03:39,960
Today I shall find my friend." Wilbur looked everywhere. He searched his pen

37
00:03:39,960 --> 00:03:49,159
thoroughly. He examined the window ledge, stared up at the ceiling, but he saw nothing new.

38
00:03:49,159 --> 00:03:54,440
Finally, he decided he would have to speak up. He hated to break the lovely

39
00:03:54,440 --> 00:03:58,760
stillness of dawn by using his voice, but he couldn't think of any other way

40
00:03:58,760 --> 00:04:07,239
to locate the mysterious new friend who was nowhere to be seen. So Wilbur cleared his throat.

41
00:04:07,239 --> 00:04:14,119
"Attention, please," he said in a loud, firm voice. Will the party who addressed

42
00:04:14,119 --> 00:04:19,800
me at bedtime last night kindly make himself or herself known by giving an

43
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:23,400
appropriate sign or signal?

44
00:04:23,400 --> 00:04:26,358
Wilbur paused and listened.

45
00:04:26,358 --> 00:04:32,679
All the other animals lifted their heads and stared at him. Wilbur blushed, but

46
00:04:32,679 --> 00:04:38,119
he was determined to get in touch with his unknown friend. "Attention, please,"

47
00:04:38,119 --> 00:04:41,159
he said. "I will repeat the message.

48
00:04:41,159 --> 00:04:46,119
Will the party who addressed me at bedtime last night kindly speak up?

49
00:04:46,119 --> 00:04:53,480
Please tell me where you are. If you are my friend, the sheep looked at each other in

50
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,079
disgust.

51
00:04:55,079 --> 00:05:01,480
Stop your nonsense, Wilbur, said the oldest sheep. If you have a new friend

52
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:07,480
here, you are probably disturbing his rest. And the quickest way to spoil a

53
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:12,519
friendship is to wake somebody up in the morning before he is ready. How can you

54
00:05:12,519 --> 00:05:16,199
be sure your friend is an early riser?

55
00:05:16,199 --> 00:05:23,719
I beg everyone's pardon, whispered Wilbur. I didn't mean to be objectionable.

56
00:05:23,719 --> 00:05:30,119
He lay down meekly in the manure facing the door. He did not know it, but his

57
00:05:30,119 --> 00:05:37,480
friend was very near, and the old sheep was right. The friend was still asleep.

58
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:42,679
Soon Lurvy appeared with slops for breakfast. Wilbur rushed out, ate

59
00:05:42,679 --> 00:05:47,239
everything in a hurry, and licked the trough. The sheep moved off down the

60
00:05:47,239 --> 00:05:53,318
lane. The gander waddled along behind them pulling grass. And then, just as

61
00:05:53,318 --> 00:05:58,838
Wilbur was settling down for his morning nap, he heard again the thin voice that

62
00:05:58,838 --> 00:06:02,838
had addressed him the night before.

63
00:06:02,838 --> 00:06:09,639
Salutations, said the voice. Wilbur jumped to his feet. Salute what? He

64
00:06:09,639 --> 00:06:13,800
cried. Salutations, repeated the voice.

65
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:16,440
What are they and where are you?

66
00:06:16,440 --> 00:06:22,920
screamed Wilbur. Please, please tell me where you are. And what are salutations?

67
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:30,199
Salutations are greetings, said the voice. When I say salutations, it's just

68
00:06:30,199 --> 00:06:36,199
my fancy way of saying hello or good morning. Actually, it's a silly

69
00:06:36,199 --> 00:06:42,039
expression, and I am surprised that I used it at all. As for my whereabouts,

70
00:06:42,039 --> 00:06:50,119
that's easy. Look up here in the corner of the doorway. Here I am. Look, I'm waving.

71
00:06:50,119 --> 00:06:56,519
At last Wilbur saw the creature that had spoken to him in such a kindly way.

72
00:06:56,519 --> 00:07:02,838
Stretched across the upper part of the doorway was a big spiderweb, and hanging

73
00:07:02,838 --> 00:07:10,199
from the top of the web, head down, was a large gray spider. She was about the

74
00:07:10,199 --> 00:07:16,119
size of a gumdrop. She had eight legs, and she was waving one of them at Wilbur

75
00:07:16,119 --> 00:07:22,039
in friendly greeting. "See me now?" she asked.

76
00:07:22,039 --> 00:07:28,599
"Oh, yes indeed," said Wilbur. "Yes, indeed. How are you? Good morning.

77
00:07:28,599 --> 00:07:30,358
Salutations.

78
00:07:30,358 --> 00:07:36,119
Very pleased to meet you." "What is your name, please? May I have your name?" "My

79
00:07:36,119 --> 00:07:41,079
name," said the spider, "is Charlotte."

80
00:07:41,079 --> 00:07:44,599
"Charlotte? What?" asked Wilbur eagerly.

81
00:07:44,599 --> 00:07:51,639
"Charlotte A. Kavatica." But just call me Charlotte. I think you're beautiful,

82
00:07:51,639 --> 00:07:58,838
said Wilbur. Well, I am pretty, replied Charlotte. There's no denying that.

83
00:07:58,838 --> 00:08:05,000
Almost all spiders are rather nicel looking. I'm not as flashy as some, but

84
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:11,159
I'll do. I wish I could see you, Wilbur, as clearly as you can see me.

85
00:08:11,159 --> 00:08:17,079
Why can't you? asked the pig. I'm right here.

86
00:08:17,079 --> 00:08:22,599
Yes, but I'm nearsighted, replied Charlotte. I've always been dreadfully

87
00:08:22,599 --> 00:08:28,599
nearsighted. It's good in some ways, not so good in others.

88
00:08:28,599 --> 00:08:31,400
Watch me wrap up this fly.

89
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:36,358
A fly that had been crawling along Wilbur's trough had flown up and

90
00:08:36,358 --> 00:08:43,000
blundered into the lower part of Charlotte's web and was tangled in the sticky threads.

91
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:51,079
The fly was beating its wings furiously, trying to break loose and free itself.

92
00:08:51,079 --> 00:08:54,760
First, said Charlotte, I dive at him.

93
00:08:54,760 --> 00:09:02,440
She plunged headirst toward the fly as she dropped a tiny silken thread unwound

94
00:09:02,440 --> 00:09:07,000
from her rear end. Next, I wrap him up.

95
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:13,239
She grabbed the fly, threw a few jets of silk around it, and rolled it over and

96
00:09:13,239 --> 00:09:17,079
over, wrapping it so that it couldn't move.

97
00:09:17,079 --> 00:09:23,480
Wilbur watched in horror. He could hardly believe what he was seeing, and

98
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:29,480
although he detested flies, he was sorry for this one.

99
00:09:29,480 --> 00:09:35,960
"There," said Charlotte. "Now I knock him out so he'll be more comfortable."

100
00:09:35,960 --> 00:09:42,199
She bit the fly. "He can't feel a thing now," she remarked. "He'll make a

101
00:09:42,199 --> 00:09:45,559
perfect breakfast for me."

102
00:09:45,559 --> 00:09:50,280
"You mean you eat flies?" gasped Wilbur.

103
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:51,880
"Certainly.

104
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:59,480
Flies, bugs, grasshoppers, choice beetles, moths, butterflies,

105
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:07,318
tasty cockroaches, gnats, midgetes, daddy longlegs, centipedes, mosquitoes,

106
00:10:07,318 --> 00:10:12,440
crickets, anything that is careless enough to get caught in my web. I have

107
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:19,559
to live, don't I? Why, yes, of course, said Wilbur. Do they taste good?

108
00:10:19,559 --> 00:10:27,880
Delicious. Of course, I don't really eat them. I drink them. Drink their blood. I

109
00:10:27,880 --> 00:10:38,358
love blood, said Charlotte, and her pleasant thin voice grew even thinner and more pleasant.

110
00:10:38,358 --> 00:10:45,159
Don't say that, groaned Wilbur. Please don't say things like that. Why not?

111
00:10:45,159 --> 00:10:51,318
It's true, and I have to say what is true. I am not entirely happy about my

112
00:10:51,318 --> 00:10:57,239
diet of flies and bugs, but it's the way I'm made. A spider has to pick up a

113
00:10:57,239 --> 00:11:03,318
living somehow or other. And I happen to be a trapper. I just naturally build a

114
00:11:03,318 --> 00:11:08,920
web and trap flies and other insects. My mother was a trapper before me. Her

115
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:15,000
mother was a trapper before her. All our family have been trappers. Way back for

116
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:22,760
thousands and thousands of years we spiders have been laying for flies and bugs.

117
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:29,400
It's a miserable inheritance, said Wilbur gloomily. He was sad because his

118
00:11:29,400 --> 00:11:32,679
new friend was so bloodthirsty.

119
00:11:32,679 --> 00:11:39,079
Yes, it is, agreed Charlotte. But I can't help it. I don't know how the

120
00:11:39,079 --> 00:11:43,800
first spider in the early days of the world happened to think up this fancy

121
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:50,039
idea of spinning a web, but she did, and it was clever of her, too. And since

122
00:11:50,039 --> 00:11:55,960
then, all of us spiders have had to work the same trick. It's not a bad pitch on

123
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:58,679
the whole.

124
00:11:58,679 --> 00:12:05,480
It's cruel, replied Wilbur, who did not intend to be argued out of his position.

125
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:08,838
Well, you can't talk, said Charlotte.

126
00:12:08,838 --> 00:12:15,000
You have your meals brought to you in a pale. Nobody feeds me. I have to get my

127
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:19,800
own living. I live by my wits. I have to

128
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:26,358
be sharp and clever, lest I go hungry. I have to think things out, catch what I

129
00:12:26,358 --> 00:12:30,519
can, take what comes.

130
00:12:30,519 --> 00:12:36,119
And it just so happens, my friend, that what comes is flies and insects and

131
00:12:36,119 --> 00:12:42,679
bugs. And furthermore, said Charlotte, shaking one of her legs, do you realize

132
00:12:42,679 --> 00:12:48,280
that if I didn't catch bugs and eat them, bugs would increase and multiply

133
00:12:48,280 --> 00:12:55,239
and get so numerous that they'd destroy the earth, wipe out everything.

134
00:12:55,239 --> 00:13:00,679
Really, said Wilbur. I wouldn't want that to happen. Perhaps your web is a

135
00:13:00,679 --> 00:13:05,960
good thing after all. The goose had been listening to this conversation and

136
00:13:05,960 --> 00:13:11,318
chuckling to herself. There are a lot of things Wilbur doesn't know about life,

137
00:13:11,318 --> 00:13:17,159
she thought. He's really a very innocent little pig. He doesn't even know what's

138
00:13:17,159 --> 00:13:21,800
going to happen to him around Christmas time.

139
00:13:21,800 --> 00:13:27,800
He has no idea that Mr. Zuckerman and Lurvy are plotting to kill him. And the

140
00:13:27,800 --> 00:13:33,239
goose raised herself a bit and poked her eggs a little further under her so that

141
00:13:33,239 --> 00:13:39,400
they would receive the full heat from her warm body and soft feathers.

142
00:13:39,400 --> 00:13:46,119
Charlotte stood quietly over the fly, preparing to eat it. Wilbur lay down and

143
00:13:46,119 --> 00:13:51,800
closed his eyes. He was tired from his wakeful night and from the excitement of

144
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:57,639
meeting someone for the first time. A breeze brought him the smell of clover,

145
00:13:57,639 --> 00:14:04,760
the sweet smelling world beyond his fence. Well, he thought, I've got a new

146
00:14:04,760 --> 00:14:10,838
friend, all right, but what a gamble friendship is. Charlotte is fierce,

147
00:14:10,838 --> 00:14:17,159
brutal, scheming, bloodthirsty, everything I don't like. How can I learn

148
00:14:17,159 --> 00:14:23,960
to like her? Even though she is pretty and of course clever.

149
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:29,239
Wilbur was merely suffering the doubts and fears that often go with finding a

150
00:14:29,239 --> 00:14:34,199
new friend. In good time he was to discover that he was mistaken about

151
00:14:34,199 --> 00:14:41,880
Charlotte. Underneath her rather bold and cruel exterior, she had a kind heart

152
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:48,280
and she was to prove loyal and true to the very end.

153
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:51,000
Chapter 6.

154
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
Summer days.
